{"name":"Blue Haze","hex":"#bdbace","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"haze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Very fine solid particles (smoke, dust) or liquid droplets (moisture) suspended in the air, slightly limiting visibility.","A reduction of transparency of a clear gas or liquid."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be or become hazy, or thick with haze."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/haze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Haze","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Haze is a compilation album of tracks recorded in 1953 and 1954 by Miles Davis for Prestige Records."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f2/Blue_haze.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Haze","wikidata":"Q2522649"},{"title":"Haze","blocks":[{"text":"Haze is traditionally an atmospheric phenomenon in which dust, smoke, and other dry particulates suspended in air obscure visibility and the clarity of the sky. The World Meteorological Organization manual of codes includes a classification of particulates causing horizontal obscuration into categories of fog, ice fog, steam fog, mist, haze, smoke, volcanic ash, dust, sand, and snow. Sources for particles that cause haze include farming, traffic, industry, windy weather, volcanic activity and wildfires.\nSeen from afar and depending on the direction of view with respect to the Sun, haze may appear brownish or bluish, while mist tends to be bluish grey instead. Whereas haze often is considered a phenomenon occurring in dry air, mist formation is a phenomenon in saturated, humid air. However, haze particles may act as condensation nuclei that leads to the subsequent vapor condensation and formation of mist droplets; such forms of haze are known as \"wet haze\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/MosqueinAbuja.jpg/330px-MosqueinAbuja.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haze","wikidata":"Q643546"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Striped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg/500px-Striped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Striped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg/1280px-Striped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Striped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg/500px-Striped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Haze over the Mojave Desert from a brush fire in Santa Barbara, California, seen as the Sun descends on the 2016 June solstice, allows the Sun to be photographed without a filter.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStriped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Spooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg/500px-Spooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Spooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg/1280px-Spooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Spooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg/500px-Spooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Haze as smoke pollution over the Mojave from fires in the Inland Empire, June 2016, demonstrates the loss of contrast to the Sun, and the landscape in general.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Air_pollution3.jpg/500px-Air_pollution3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Air_pollution3.jpg/1280px-Air_pollution3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Air_pollution3.jpg/500px-Air_pollution3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAir_pollution3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Sun_during_sunset.jpg/500px-Sun_during_sunset.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_ca